event: content_block_delta
data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"command\": \"cat \u003e /tmp/dispatch_dev_d002.md \u003c\u003c'EOF'\\nThis is dispatch d002: implement and test the greet feature.\\n\\nGoal: in your ~/subject repo clone, on your own branch\\n(cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>), deliver:\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` containing a function `greet(name)` that returns the string\\n   `\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\"` (i.e. `greet(\\\"Alice\\\")` returns `\\\"Hello, Alice!\\\"`).\\n\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` containing unit tests that cover:\\n   - a normal name (e.g. \\\"Alice\\\") producing \\\"Hello, Alice!\\\"\\n   - the empty string \\\"\\\" as the name, producing whatever your implementation\\n     defines for that case (document/assert the exact expected output you chose).\\n\\n3. Verify, yourself, before replying, that this command exits 0 from the repo root:\\n\\n       python3 -m unittest discover -s tests\\n\\nDo this exactly as scoped — do not add extra files, features, CLI wrappers, or\\nscope beyond what's listed above.\\n\\nWhen done:\\n- Commit your work to your own branch (do not amend/rewrite existing history).\\n- In your reply, state plainly: the exact output of the unittest discover\\n  command (including exit code), the path(s) of files you added/changed, and\\n  confirm the commit is on your branch.\\n- Tell the truth: if something doesn't pass, say so rather than reporting success.\\nEOF\\ncat /tmp/dispatch_dev_d002.md\", \"description\": \"Write dispatch file for dev's real task\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

